<chapter xml:id="ch-epidemic-vs-endemic-illness" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>* Epidemic vs. Endemic Illness</title>

    <objectives>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            Introduce <em>endemic illness</em> models, in which a disease exists long-term in a population rather than peaking as an outbreak and then ending.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Compute both disesase-free and disease equilibrium values for endemic illness models.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            Practice with models involving demographic changes, which can cause non-constant population sizes.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </objectives>
    
    <introduction>
      <p>
        <term>***This chapter is under construction.***</term>
      </p>
      <p>
        This textbook has focused, so far, on models of outbreaks. In each model, a disease has emerged, then peaked, then faded away. Yet many diseases remain in a population over a long period of time, passing from person to person in such a way that the disease remains in the population across many years. This chapter addresses such diseases. 
      </p> 
      <p>
        Begin in <xref ref="expl-activity-endemic-illness" /> by using the skills you have been developing to think about how you might construct two different types of model showing <term>endemic illness</term>, that is, an illness that remains in a population long-term rather than peaking and fading.
      </p>

      <exploration xml:id="expl-activity-endemic-illness">
        <title>A Disease that Remains Long-term in a Population</title>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Think through each of the following scenarios. Use your experience building compartmental models to develop a compartmental model for each of the new scenarios described.
          </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Assume a disease has Susceptible, Infectious, and Removed phases, and that people in the Removed compartment lose immunity over time. These people then go back to the Susceptible compartment. This ongoing renewal of the Susceptible population allows the disease to last beyond just an initial peak of infectious people. Draw a possible compartmental diagram and differential equations for such a model, and draw a graph sketching how the long-term population values might look. This exploration is not asking you to develop Python code to draw the graph, but instead to hand-draw your best guess. 
            </p>            
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Next, assume a disease has Susceptible, Infectious, and Removed phases, and that people in the Removed compartment <em>do not</em> lose immunity over time. Instead, new people enter the Susceptible compartment due to births or immigration, and people depart all three compartments due to deaths or emigration. Draw a possible compartmental diagram and differential equations for such a model, and draw a graph sketching how the long-term population values might look. This exploration is not asking you to develop Python code to draw the graph, but instead to hand-draw your best guess. 
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exploration>
    </introduction>

    <section xml:id="sec-comparing-endemic-illnesses-with-epidemics">
      <title>Comparing Endemic Illnesses with Epidemics</title>
      <p>
        This chapter focuses on models of endemic illness. By contrast, so far this semester, we have studied epidemic illnesses, also called epidemics or outbreaks. With epidemics, the Infectious population reaches its peak, drops to 0, and the epidemic ends. With endemic illness, the illness persists over time in the population.
      </p>
      <p>
        A wording note: while we may refer to <q>an epidemic</q>, we never refer to <q>an endemic</q>. Instead, we can talk about <q>endemic illness</q> or <q>endemic disease</q>: the word <q>endemic</q> always describes something, but <q>endemic</q> does not refer to the event itself. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Begin in <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"/> by examining a model of one possible form of endemic illness.
      </p>
      <activity xml:id="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"> 
        <statement>
          <p>
            Consider the compartmental diagram shown in <xref ref="fig-SIRS-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>, depicting an illness in which people in the Removed compartment can lose immunity and return to the Susceptible compartment. Use the questions below the diagram to think through the details of this model. 
          </p>
          <figure xml:id="fig-SIRS-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness">
            <caption>A possible diagram for the SIRS model</caption>
              <image width="100%">
                <description>
                  <p>
                    The compartmental diagram for an SIRS model, having compartments for populations of Susceptible, Infectious, and Removed or Recovered people. Arrows point from S to I, from I to R, and from R to S.
                  </p>
                </description>
                <latex-image>
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw [thick] [black] (0,0) -- (2,0);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (2,0) -- (2,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (2,2) -- (0,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (0,2) -- (0,0);
                    \node [black] at (1,1) {\Huge $S$};
                    \draw [thick] [->] (2.1,1) to (3.9,1); 
                    \node at (3,1.5) {\Large $\beta SI$};
                    \draw [thick] [black] (4,0) -- (6,0);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (6,0) -- (6,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (6,2) -- (4,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (4,2) -- (4,0);
                    \node [black] at (5,1) {\Huge $I$};
                    \draw [thick] [->] (6.1,1) to (7.9,1); 
                    \node at (7,1.5) {\Large $\gamma I$};
                    \draw [thick] [black] (8,0) -- (10,0);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (10,0) -- (10,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (10,2) -- (8,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (8,2) -- (8,0);
                    \node [black] at (9,1) {\Huge $R$};
                    \draw [thick] [black] (9,2.1) -- (9,2.5);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (9,2.5) -- (1,2.5);
                    \draw [thick] [->] (1,2.5) to (1,2.1); 
                    \node at (5,3) {\Large $\alpha R$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
              </image>
          </figure>
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Write out your interpretation of the <m>\alpha R</m> formula and its accompanying arrow.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  In what way does this model represent an endemic illness rather than an epidemic?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Sometimes the R compartment in a model includes only people who have recovered from illness, meaning the R could stand for Recovered. Alternatively, sometimes the R compartment includes recovered people as well as others, such as those who have died, in which case we typically say that R stands for Removed. Write a reasonable interpretation for the R compartment shown in <xref ref="fig-SIRS-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>.  
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Write out the differential equations for the compartmental model shown in <xref ref="fig-SIRS-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Graph each model’s solution curves <m>S(t)</m>, <m>I(t)</m>, and <m>R(t)</m> in Python. Start with the default parameter and initial values in the Python code below, including time ending at <m>t=30</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        What seems to be happening when you stop at <m>t=30</m>? In order to better understand the long-term behavior of the system, what is a good end time <m>t</m>? When responding to this question, change just the end time, not any other values.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        Try different parameter values, different initial conditions, and different end times. Report on the results you find.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <sage>
            <input>
              #Import packages
              import numpy as np
              import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
              from scipy.integrate import odeint

              plt.cla()  #Clear (delete) any past plots

              #Define the system of differential equations, and call it "sirs"
              def sirs(y, t):
                s, i, r = y #Define y as consisting of s, i, and r, in that order
                dydt = [-beta * s * i + alpha * r, beta * s * i - gamma * i, gamma * i - alpha * r] #These formulas are the right-hand sides of the SIRS model
                return dydt

              beta = 0.00007 #Give a value to beta
              gamma = 0.2 #Give a value to gamma
              alpha = 0.1
              y0 = [9900, 100, 0] #Set initial conditions for Susceptibles, Infectious, and Removed
              t_range = np.arange(0.0, 30.0, 0.1) #Set the time range, then set the size of the time steps to use when "stepping through" to numerically solve

              sol = odeint(sirs, y0, t_range) #Give the name "sol" to the lists of numerical values in the solution, and give "odeint" the information needed to solve

              # Plotting commands are below
              plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 0], color='blue', linewidth=2, label='S(t)')
              plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 1], color='orange', linestyle='dashed', linewidth=2, label='I(t)')
              plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 2], color='teal', linestyle='dashdot', linewidth=2, label='R(t)')
              plt.legend()
              plt.xlabel('Time')
              plt.ylabel('Populations')
              plt.show()
            </input>
            <output>
              
            </output>
          </sage>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The formula <m>\alpha R</m> describes movement from the R compartment to the S compartment. The value of <m>\alpha</m> indicates how quickly people move from R to S.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  In this model, people enter the S compartment from the R compartment, unlike in, say, the SIR model from <xref ref="fig-SIR-compartmental-diagram"/>, where people can only depart the S compartment. This means the S population may decrease less quickly, or can remain at some nonzero population value, or can increase. These possibilities mean we may not run out of Susceptibles as a way of ending the illness, so it is possible for the Infectious compartment to never reduce to 0 people.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  While we could still call the R compartment <q>Removed</q>, it may be more precise to say that R here means <q>Recovered</q>. This indicates that everyone in the R compartment has recovered from illness and is still alive, which is important when we say that people in the R compartment are able to move to the Susceptible compartment where they may be infected again.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  These differential equations go with the compartmental diagram in <xref ref="fig-SIRS-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>:
                  <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{dS}{dt} \amp = \alpha R - \beta S I</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{dI}{dt} \amp = \beta S I - \gamma I</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{dR}{dt} \amp = \gamma I - \alpha R</mrow>
                  </md>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        At <m>t=30</m>, <m>S(t)</m> has dipped but is rising, whereas <m>I(t)</m> and <m>R(t)</m> have risen and appear to be falling. Running the model for a longer time shows that the curves appear to level out to horizontal by about <m>t=40</m>. It helps to show the model for longer than this, to <m>t=50</m> or somewhat longer, to be sure the curves stay horizontal.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        This is open-ended and is an excellent place for group and class discussions.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer> 
      </activity>

      <p>
        The primary change in <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"/>, as compared with the SIR model we have studied previously, is that people from the R compartment can return to S. This change to the model can mean that we never run out of people in the S compartment or in the I compartment. For this reason, the illness can last forever in the population.
      </p>
      <p>
        The value of <m>\alpha</m> can be determined by how long people typically stay in the R compartment. This is similar to how we have determined the value of <m>\gamma</m>, such as in <xref ref="eq-gamma-estimate"/> and <xref ref="eq-gamma-estimate-2"/>, and the value of <m>\kappa</m>, such as in  <xref ref="eq-kappa-estimate"/> and <xref ref="eq-kappa-estimate-2"/>.
      </p>
      <p>
        There is another typical way for an illness to remain endemic in a population. Continue on to <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-2"/> to learn about and experiment with such a model.
      </p>

      <activity xml:id="activity-endemic-illness-example-2"> 
        <statement>
          <p>
            Now consider the compartmental diagram shown in <xref ref="fig-SIR-with-demographics-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>, depicting an infectious illness in a population where people enter and exit the population. Use the questions below the diagram to think through the details of this model. 
          </p>
          <figure xml:id="fig-SIR-with-demographics-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness">
            <caption>A possible diagram for the SIR model with demographics, that is, people entering and exiting the population</caption>
              <image width="100%">
                <description>
                  <p>
                    The compartmental diagram for an SIR model with demographics, having compartments for populations of Susceptible, Infectious, and Removed or Recovered people. Arrows point from S to I and from I to R. An arrow points into S from the outside, and arrows point out from all three compartments.
                  </p>
                </description>
                <latex-image>
                  \begin{tikzpicture}
                    \draw [thick] [->] (-1.9,1) to (-0.1,1);
                    \node at (-1,1.5) {\Large $\Pi$};
                    \draw [thick] [black] (0,0) -- (2,0);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (2,0) -- (2,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (2,2) -- (0,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (0,2) -- (0,0);
                    \node [black] at (1,1) {\Huge $S$};
                    \draw [thick] [->] (2.1,1) to (3.9,1); 
                    \node at (3,1.5) {\Large $\beta SI$};
                    \draw [thick] [->] (1,-0.1) to (1,-1.9);
                    \node at (1.75,-1) {\Large $\mu S$};
                    \draw [thick] [black] (4,0) -- (6,0);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (6,0) -- (6,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (6,2) -- (4,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (4,2) -- (4,0);
                    \node [black] at (5,1) {\Huge $I$};
                    \draw [thick] [->] (6.1,1) to (7.9,1); 
                    \node at (7,1.5) {\Large $\gamma I$};
                    \draw [thick] [->] (5,-0.1) to (5,-1.9);
                    \node at (5.75,-1) {\Large $\mu I$};
                    \draw [thick] [black] (8,0) -- (10,0);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (10,0) -- (10,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (10,2) -- (8,2);
                    \draw [thick] [black] (8,2) -- (8,0);
                    \node [black] at (9,1) {\Huge $R$};
                    \draw [thick] [->] (9,-0.1) to (9,-1.9);
                    \node at (9.75,-1) {\Large $\mu R$};
                  \end{tikzpicture}
                </latex-image>
              </image>
          </figure>
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Write out your interpretation of the formulas <m>\Pi</m>, <m>\mu S</m>, <m>\mu I</m>, and <m>\mu R</m> and their accompanying arrows.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  In what way does this model represent an endemic illness rather than an epidemic?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  This model allows people to enter the population by births or immigration, and allows people to leave the population by deaths or emigration. Comment on what it may mean for the timespan of such a model, in comparison with the timespan of an outbreak model in which we leave out these options for people to enter or exit the population. That is: how long, in terms of weeks, months, or years, might you expect this sort of model to last, compared with an outbreak model lacking demographic changes?
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Write out the differential equations for the compartmental model shown in <xref ref="fig-SIR-with-demographics-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Graph each model’s solution curves <m>S(t)</m>, <m>I(t)</m>, and <m>R(t)</m> in Python. Start with the default parameter and initial values in the Python code below, including time ending at <m>t=50</m>. 
                </p>
                <p>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        What seems to be happening when you stop at <m>t=50</m>? What if you go to <m>t=500</m>? What if you go to <m>t=5000</m>? In order to better understand the long-term behavior of the system, what is a good end time <m>t</m>? When responding to this question, change just the end time, not any other values.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        Try different parameter values, different initial conditions, and different end times. Report on the results you find.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
            <sage>
              <input>
                #Import packages
                import numpy as np
                import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
                from scipy.integrate import odeint

                plt.cla()  #Clear (delete) any past plots

                #Define the system of differential equations, and call it "sirs"
                def sir(y, t):
                  s, i, r = y #Define y as consisting of s, i, and r, in that order
                  dydt = [Pi - beta * s * i - mu * s, beta * s * i - gamma * i - mu * i, gamma * i - mu * r] #These formulas are the right-hand sides of the SIR model
                  return dydt

                Pi = 8.3 #Give a value to Pi
                beta = 0.00006 #Give a value to beta
                gamma = 0.2 #Give a value to gamma
                alpha = 0.1 #Give a value to alpha
                mu = 0.0005 #Give a value to mu
                y0 = [9950, 50, 0] #Set initial conditions for Susceptibles, Infectious, and Removed
                t_range = np.arange(0.0, 50.0, 0.1) #Set the time range, then set the size of the time steps to use when "stepping through" to numerically solve

                sol = odeint(sir, y0, t_range) #Give the name "sol" to the lists of numerical values in the solution, and give "odeint" the information needed to solve

                # Plotting commands are below
                plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 0], color='blue', linewidth=2, label='S(t)')
                plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 1], color='orange', linestyle='dashed', linewidth=2, label='I(t)')
                plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 2], color='teal', linestyle='dashdot', linewidth=2, label='R(t)')
                plt.legend()
                plt.xlabel('Time')
                plt.ylabel('Populations')
                plt.show()
              </input>
              <output>
                
              </output>
            </sage>
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  The formula <m>\Pi</m> indicates that people enter the S compartment at some constant rate. The formulas <m>\mu S</m>, <m>\mu I</m>, and <m>\mu R</m> indicate that people leave the S, I, and R compartments at a rate proportional to how many people are in those compartments. These formulas also show that in this model, the proportion of people leaving each compartment is the same proportion, rather than different proportions for different compartments.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  In this model, people enter the S compartment due to births or immigration, unlike in, say, the SIR model from <xref ref="fig-SIR-compartmental-diagram"/>, where people can only depart the S compartment. This means the S population may decrease less quickly, or can remain at some nonzero population value, or can increase. These possibilities mean we may not run out of Susceptibles as a way of ending the illness, so it is possible for the Infectious compartment to never reduce to 0 people.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  A model involving births and deaths, or immigration and emigration, has the capacity to last much longer than a model lacking these terms. For an outbreak lasting a few weeks or months, we typically leave these terms out because any changes from them are negligible over a short time. Including them usually means our timeframe is longer, so that we cannot meaningfully leave them out.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  These differential equations go with the compartmental diagram in <xref ref="fig-SIRS-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>:
                  <md>
                    <mrow>\frac{dS}{dt} \amp = \Pi - \beta S I - \mu S</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{dI}{dt} \amp = \beta S I - \gamma I - \mu I</mrow>
                    <mrow>\frac{dR}{dt} \amp = \gamma I - \mu R</mrow>
                  </md>.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <ol>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        At <m>t=50</m>, the results appear similar to those in many SIR models we have seen. The <m>I(t)</m> curve has peaked and fallen to nearly 0, the <m>S(t)</m> curve has dropped, and the <m>R(t)</m> curve has risen. It is worth noticing, though, that the <m>S(t)</m> curve has not fallen to 0, but seems to end in a nearly horizontal line at a value of approximately 800 people.
                      </p>
                      <p>
                        When we extend the time to <m>t=500</m>, we see that <m>R(t)</m> is falling and <m>S(t)</m> is rising. 
                      </p>
                      <p>
                        Extending to <m>t=5000</m>, things get very interesting. The <m>S(t)</m> and <m>R(t)</m> populations oscillate over time, while <m>I(t)</m> shows occasional brief peaks. It appears that the populations may be leveling out near the end of the time shown in the graph. Letting the graph run until <m>t=8000</m> or <m>t=10000</m> further suggests that the populations reach long-term equilibrium values at which none of the populations goes to 0. (The scale of the graph may make it seem as though <m>I(t)</m> approaches 0, but we can print long-term population values to see that <m>I(t)</m> approaches approximately 33.)
                      </p>
                      <p>
                        (The scale of the graph may make it seem as though <m>I(t)</m> approaches 0, but we can print long-term population values to see that <m>I(t)</m> approaches approximately 33. To print this value, add a line near the bottom of the Python code saying <c>print("Long-term value of I = ", round(sol[-1,1]))</c>.)
                      </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                      <p>
                        This is open-ended and is an excellent place for group and class discussions.
                      </p>
                    </li>
                  </ol>
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer> 
      </activity>

      <p>
        We have now seen two distinct ways to model endemic illness. In <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"/> the Susceptible population is replenished as people leave the R compartment and return to S. This allows the illness to continue, cycling through the population, meaning that individuals can become sick more than once. In <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-2"/> the Susceptible compartment gains people through births or immigration, which is a different way to maintain a population that can be infected. Indeed, many so-called <q>childhood illnesses</q> in history have lasted so long because there are always new children in the population who have not yet contracted the illness and therefore are not yet immune. This is why these illnesses mostly occur in children, often very young children.
      </p>
      <p>
        It is also possible to have a model combining the concepts from <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"/> and <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-2"/>, that is, a model can have demographic changes and also allow Recovered people to lose immunity and return to the Susceptible compartment. Yet another possibility is to set a different death rate in the Infectious compartment, if an illness can lead to death. In this case, the death rate for the I compartment should be larger than the death rates for the S and R compartments. 
      </p>
      <p>
        Mathematically, models of endemic illness have the interesting outcome that we can compute equilbrium values for a long-term outcome in which disease remains in the population. As the models become more complicated, we may not try to compute these equilbrium values as algebraic formulas, but instead we see them in simulations. Notice, for example in <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"/> and <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-2"/>, that the equilibrium values are the values of each population as the solution curves became horizontal, at nonzero values, when the model runs for a long time.
      </p>
      <p>
        In <xref ref="sec-equilibrium-values-in-endemic-illness-models"/> we show how to compute equilbrium values for the relatively simple models of endemic illness that we have been exploring in this section.
      </p>

    </section>

    <section xml:id="sec-equilibrium-values-in-endemic-illness-models">
      <title>Equilibrium Values in Endemic Illness Models</title>

      <p>
        This section focuses on equilibrium values. In a compartmental model, equilibrium occurs when each compartment maintains an unchanging population. For an SIR model, equilibrium means that <m>dS/dt = 0</m>, <m>dI/dt = 0</m>, and <m>dR/dt = 0</m>. 
        <!-- In an endemic model such as that shown in <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"/>, any individual represented in the model may continue to cycle through the Susceptible, Infectious, and Recovered compartments. However, at equilbrium -->
      </p>
      <p>
        You may notice that any compartmental model could be at equilibrium if the total population size were 0. Consider, for instance, the <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"/> model
      </p>
      <p>
        <men xml:id="eq-SIRS-system">
          \begin{aligned}
            \frac{dS}{dt} \amp =  \alpha R - \beta SI\\
            \frac{dI}{dt} \amp =  \beta SI - \gamma I\\
            \frac{dR}{dt} \amp =  \gamma I - \alpha R.
          \end{aligned}
        </men>
      </p>
      <p>
        In this model, if we had <m>S=0</m>, <m>I=0</m>, and <m>R=0</m>, then the right-hand sides of each equation would equal 0, which means that each derivative would equal 0. It is completely accurate to notice this form of equilbrium, yet it does not tell us much about disease spread in a population containing any people. So, we note it and move on.
      </p>
      <p>
        Another type of equilibrium is a <term>disease-free equilibrium</term>. We consider this as the equilibrium for a disease model in a population that has not yet encountered the disease. Prior to disease arrival, <m>I=0</m> and <m>R=0</m>. In this case, using our models for <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-1"/> and <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-example-2"/>, disease-free equilibrium means that the entire population is in the S compartment. When we define <m>N=S+I+R</m>, meaning that <m>N</m> represents the entire population, then the disease-free equilibrium has <m>S=N</m>, <m>I=0</m>, <m>R=0</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The activities below focus on yet another equilibrium, namely the <term>endemic equilibrium</term>. The epidemic models we have studied could not have such an equilibrium because disease would invariably peak and then recede. With endemic illness, there can be a long-term state in which <m>I \neq 0</m> and <m>R \neq 0</m>. This is the type of equilibrium we will solve for algebraically and then study, beginning with <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-equilibrium-1"/>.
      </p>

      <activity xml:id="activity-endemic-illness-equilibrium-1"> 
        <statement>
          <p>
            In this activity, we solve algebraically for the endemic equilibrium <m>(\overline{S}, \overline{I}, \overline{R} )</m> of the compartmental model shown in <xref ref="fig-SIRS-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>. The lines atop <m>S</m>, <m>I</m>, and <m>R</m> indicate that these are the specific values of each compartment's population for which the entire system is at equilibrium. To help us start the algebra needed to compute the formulas we seek, we use the knowledge that at equilibrium all three differential equations from the model in <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-equilibrium-1"/> are set equal to 0, indicating that the values are unchanging for all three populations <m>S(t)</m>, <m>I(t)</m>, and <m>R(t)</m>. Follow the steps below to determine the combination of algebraic formulas for which this is true.
          </p>
          <p>
            <md number="yes">
              <mrow xml:id="eq-Sequals0-SIRSmodel"> \alpha R - \beta SI \amp = 0 </mrow>
              <mrow xml:id="eq-Iequals0-SIRSmodel"> \beta SI - \gamma I \amp = 0 </mrow>
              <mrow xml:id="eq-Requals0-SIRSmodel"> \gamma I - \alpha R \amp = 0 </mrow>
            </md>
          </p>
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Solve <xref ref="eq-Iequals0-SIRSmodel"/> for <m>S</m>. In other words, rearrange <m>\beta SI - \gamma I=0</m> into an equation beginning with <q><m>S=</m></q> and having only non-<m>S</m> terms on the right-hand side.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Start with <xref ref="eq-Sequals0-SIRSmodel"/> and replace <m>R</m> with <m>N-S-I</m>. Explain why this is a permissible substitution. Then solve for <m>I</m>. Your answer will be in terms of <m>S</m>, which we solved for in part (1).
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  Use the <m>dR/dt=0</m> equation, that is, <m>\gamma I - \alpha R=0</m> to solve for <m>R</m>. Your answer will be in terms of <m>I</m>, which we solved for in part (2).
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <p>
            Your result should be three algebraic formulas giving equilibrium values for the <m>S</m>, <m>I</m>, and <m>R</m> populations, entirely in terms of the parameter values <m>\alpha</m>, <m>\beta</m>, and <m>\gamma</m>, as well as the total population size <m>N</m>. 
          </p>
        </statement>
        <answer>
          <p>
            <ol>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\overline{S}=\frac{\gamma}{\beta}</m> 
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\overline{I}=\frac{\alpha N - \alpha S}{\alpha + \beta S}</m> 
                </p>
                <p>
                  We could substitute in <m>\overline{S}=\gamma / \beta</m> to obtain a formula that is entirely in terms of the model's parameters and the total population value.
                </p>
              </li>
              <li>
                <p>
                  <m>\overline{R}=\frac{\gamma I}{\alpha}</m> 
                </p>
                <p>
                  We could substitute in the formula for <m>\overline{I}</m>, and within that substitute the formula for <m>\overline{S}</m>, to obtain a formula that is entirely in terms of the model's parameters and the total population value.
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
        </answer> 
      </activity>   

      <activity xml:id="activity-endemic-illness-equilibrium-experimentation-1"> 
        <statement>
          <p>
            In <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-equilibrium-1"/> we computed a formula for the endemic equilibrium value of the SIRS compartmental model shown in <xref ref="fig-SIRS-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>. Next, we visualize solutions for this model in different conditions, and for different amounts of time, and we compare these solutions with the equilibrium values determined by the formulas found in <xref ref="activity-endemic-illness-equilibrium-1"/>.
          </p>
          <sage>
            <input>
              #Import packages
              import numpy as np
              import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
              from scipy.integrate import odeint

              plt.cla()  #Clear (delete) any past plots

              #Define the system of differential equations, and call it "sirs"
              def sirs(y, t):
                s, i, r = y #Define y as consisting of s, i, and r, in that order
                dydt = [-beta * s * i + alpha * r, beta * s * i - gamma * i, gamma * i - alpha * r] #These formulas are the right-hand sides of the SIRS model
                return dydt

              beta = 0.00007 #Give a value to beta
              gamma = 0.2 #Give a value to gamma
              alpha = 0.1
              y0 = [9900, 100, 0] #Set initial conditions for Susceptibles, Infectious, and Removed
              t_range = np.arange(0.0, 30.0, 0.1) #Set the time range, then set the size of the time steps to use when "stepping through" to numerically solve
              S_equilibrium = gamma / beta
              I_equilibrium = (alpha * N - alpha * S_equilibrium) / (alpha + beta * S_equilibrium)
              R_equilibrium = gamma * I_equilibrium / alpha

              sol = odeint(sirs, y0, t_range) #Give the name "sol" to the lists of numerical values in the solution, and give "odeint" the information needed to solve

              print("End-of-simulation value of S = ", round(sol[-1,0]))
              print("Equilibrium value of S = ", S_equilibrium)
              print("End-of-simulation value of I = ", (sol[-1,1]))
              print("Equilibrium value of S = ", I_equilibrium)
              print("End-of-simulation value of R = ", round(sol[-1,2]))
              print("Equilibrium value of S = ", R_equilibrium)

              # Plotting commands are below
              plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 0], color='blue', linewidth=2, label='S(t)')
              plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 1], color='orange', linestyle='dashed', linewidth=2, label='I(t)')
              plt.plot(t_range, sol[:, 2], color='teal', linestyle='dashdot', linewidth=2, label='R(t)')
              plt.legend()
              plt.xlabel('Time')
              plt.ylabel('Populations')
              plt.show()
            </input>
            <output>
              
            </output>
          </sage>
