    </section>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises-ch-endemic-illness">
      <exercise number="1">
        <p>
          Show the steps for how to solve algebraically for the endemic equilibrium <m>(\overline{S}, \overline{I}, \overline{R} )</m> of the SIR model with demographics as shown in <xref ref="fig-SIR-with-demographics-compartmental-diagram-endemic-illness"/>. Start by setting equal to 0 the differential equations for the model: 
        </p>
        <p>
          <md number="yes">
            <mrow xml:id="eq-Sequals0-SIRmodel-with-demographics"> \Pi - \beta SI - \mu S \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eq-Iequals0-SIRmodel-with-demographics"> \beta SI - \gamma I - \mu I \amp = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow xml:id="eq-Requals0-SIRmodel-with-demographics"> \gamma I - \mu R \amp = 0 </mrow>
          </md>.
        </p>
        <p>
          This indicates that the values are unchanging for all three populations <m>S(t)</m>, <m>I(t)</m>, and <m>R(t)</m>. Then follow the steps below.
        </p>
        <p>
          <ol>
            <li>
              <p>
                Solve <xref ref="eq-Iequals0-SIRmodel-with-demographics"/> for <m>S</m>. This result is the equilibrium value <m>\overline{S}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Substitute <m>\overline{S}</m> into <xref ref="eq-Sequals0-SIRmodel-with-demographics"/> and solve for <m>I</m>. This result is the equilibrium value <m>\overline{I}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <p>
                Solve <xref ref="eq-Requals0-SIRmodel-with-demographics"/> for <m>R</m>. The result contains <m>I</m>. When <m>I=\overline{I}</m>, this formula is the equilibrium value <m>\overline{R}</m>.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </p>
        <p>
          Your result should be three algebraic formulas giving equilibrium values for the <m>S</m>, <m>I</m>, and <m>R</m> populations, entirely in terms of the parameter values <m>\beta</m>, <m>\gamma</m>, <m>\mu</m>, and <m>\Pi</m>. 
        </p>
      </exercise>
      
    </exercises>

  

  </chapter>